\f0  Panel 4\
Use the attached contact sheet as the visual reference for the shot order and composition. Animate Panel 3 followed by Panel 4 as two distinct cinematic shots.\
Continue with the same Japanese couple, the same yukata, and the same summer festival. It is now darker, with warm paper lanterns against a deep blue night sky. They are moving toward the riverside fireworks viewing area.\
SHOT 1: The couple walks together through a crowded festival street. Visitors naturally pass between them. The crowd briefly obscures their view of each other and creates a small gap. The man takes one step forward, notices that the woman is no longer beside him, and stops. He turns back. She looks through the crowd and finds his eyes. Their expressions shift from momentary uncertainty to quiet recognition. Do not make either character panic.\
SHOT 2: The man gently extends one hand toward her. After a short, natural hesitation, the woman places her hand in his. He closes his fingers around hers with a soft, reassuring gesture. Show the physical contact clearly and naturally. Cut from a close-up of their hands to her face as she looks up at him with a restrained smile. He returns the smile.\
Keep the crowd moving naturally behind them while the couple's gesture remains the emotional focus. Use a subtle camera push-in and warm lantern bokeh. No dramatic slow motion, no sudden embrace, no kiss, and no exaggerated facial expressions.\
End with the couple standing close together, hands firmly but gently joined, facing in the direction of the riverside. Their hands remain joined for the following clip.\
Cinematic realism, realistic anatomy and fabric, natural festival ambience, distant crowd chatter and footsteps. Music becomes slightly warmer and more intimate. No dialogue, no subtitles, no text overlays, no changes to clothing or appearance.\
