Contact Sheet: Panel 5\
Use Panel 5 of the attached contact sheet as the composition and emotional reference. Create one continuous cinematic shot without scene cuts.\
The same Japanese couple stands side by side at the riverside fireworks viewing area at night, still holding hands. Their facial appearance, hairstyles, yukata, and relative heights remain consistent. The dark river lies behind them, with distant festival lights along the opposite bank.\
Begin with a medium two-shot from a slight side angle. A golden firework rises and bursts overhead, producing a believable warm flash across their faces and clothing. The woman looks upward, absorbed by the display. The man quietly turns his eyes toward her face instead of looking at the sky. Hold this difference in gaze long enough for the audience to notice. The woman senses his attention and turns toward him. Their eyes meet, and she gives him a small, genuine smile. At that moment, a larger golden firework blossoms in the sky behind them, creating the emotional visual payoff.\
The camera makes a slow, controlled push-in, keeping both faces readable and the fireworks visible in the background. Their expressions remain subtle and natural. Their hands stay joined throughout the shot. Firework light should change naturally with each burst, with realistic highlights and shadows rather than constant artificial illumination.\
End on their shared gaze as the golden fireworks expand overhead. Do not introduce a kiss or a new action. Preserve the intimate pause.\
Cinematic realism, realistic night exposure, physically plausible fireworks and reflections, distant firework reports, a gradual musical swell synchronized with the largest burst. No dialogue, no subtitles, no text overlays, no sudden camera movement, no identity or costume drift.\
